issue.body:
## Summary

When magpie relays a tool-bearing request to an **Antigravity subscription**, Google rejects the whole turn at request validation (HTTP 400) because a tool's JSON Schema `enum` contains an empty string:

```text
Antigravity: * GenerateContentRequest.tools[0].function_declarations[24].parameters.properties[StatusMessage].properties[operationStatus].enum[3]: cannot be empty
* GenerateContentRequest.tools[0].function_declarations[129].parameters.properties[StatusMessage].properties[operationStatus].enum[3]: cannot be empty
* GenerateContentRequest.tools[0].function_declarations[234].parameters.properties[StatusMessage].properties[operationStatus].enum[3]: cannot be empty
* GenerateContentRequest.tools[0].function_declarations[339].parameters.properties[StatusMessage].properties[operationStatus].enum[3]: cannot be empty
* GenerateContentRequest.tools[0].function_declarations[474].parameters.properties[StatusMessage].properties[operationStatus].enum[3]: cannot be empty
* GenerateContentRequest.tools[0].function_declarations[607].parameters.properties[StatusMessage].properties[operationStatus].enum[3]: cannot be empty
```

(The reporter's paste was truncated at the fourth line; the full upstream message has six lines. An earlier turn with a slightly larger tool set reported indices 36 / 141 / 246 / 351 / 486 / 619 instead.)

The failing node is a client/MCP tool property `StatusMessage.operationStatus` whose enum has an empty string at index 3. Nothing reaches the model: this is a schema-validation 400, so retrying, switching accounts or waiting out a quota window cannot help.

## What actually happened (magpie routing log + dsh session records)

**Setup**: DeepSeek Harness (`dsh`, desktop on macOS) → magpie gateway → a routing group → `antigravity/gemini-3.8-flash` (Antigravity subscription). magpie v0.1.2945.

**Timeline**: five main requests within roughly 15 minutes, all HTTP 400 in under 2 s. In the dsh session transcripts each is `turn 1, step 1` with `inputTokens: 0`: **the very first model call of a brand-new session fails**, before any output. The prompts ranged from a substantive code-review request to a two-character greeting — content is irrelevant; even the greeting fails.

**Request shape**: ~280k–300k prompt tokens, of which **~274k–280k tokens are tool definitions**. Tool sources: six instances of the same Portainer MCP server (one with 239 tools, five with ~105 each), two small MCP servers (24 and 6 tools), plus dsh built-ins — 600+ `functionDeclarations`.

**Control experiment**: in the *same millisecond* as each failed request, magpie logs a separate `effort=low` request (session-title generation, 137–172 tokens, **no tools**) which returns **200**. So only tool-bearing requests fail; `effort` unset and `effort=high` fail identically. Switching the route to a non-Antigravity model works — the workaround the reporter landed on.

**The exact offending schema**, read back from the dsh session record (Portainer's OpenAPI `Endpoint` definition):

```json
"StatusMessage": {
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "operationStatus": {
      "allOf": [{
        "enum": ["processing", "warning", "error", ""],
        "type": "string",
        "x-enum-varnames": [
          "EndpointOperationStatusProcessing",
          "EndpointOperationStatusWarning",
          "EndpointOperationStatusError",
          "EndpointOperationStatusDone"
        ]
      }],
      "description": "'', 'error', 'processing'",
      "example": "error"
    }
  }
}
```

`""` is a legitimate member of Portainer's `EndpointOperationStatus` enum (`Done` = no operation in progress); the description even quotes it. The tool is well-formed JSON Schema — it is invalid only under Google's `Schema` proto.

## Root cause

On `origin/main` (`0f1b40bbc413`, byte-identical in the reporter's `v0.1.2945` / `43849d51c327`), `buildCodeAssistSent` sends every Antigravity tool declaration through `plainSchema`:

```go
// internal/gateway/codeassist.go:515-519
if ag {
    d["parameters"] = plainSchema(schema)
} else {
    d["parametersJsonSchema"] = schema
}
```

`plainSchema` already cuts a schema down to the subset Gemini/Code Assist accepts, but its `enum` case only checks the *type* of each member, never its *value*:

```go
// internal/gateway/codeassist.go:1115-1127
case "enum":
    // Google takes string enums only
    if es, ok := v.([]any); ok {
        strs := make([]any, 0, len(es))
        for _, e := range es {
            if s, ok := e.(string); ok {
                strs = append(strs, s)      // "" is a string, so it survives
            }
        }
        if len(strs) == len(es) {
            out[k] = strs                   // ["queued","running","done",""] relayed as-is
        }
    }
```

Google's `Schema` proto validates that enum values are non-empty, and reports the offending index as `enum[3]: cannot be empty`. Google's own forum reply on the same class of error describes it as "an empty string (`" "`) in their enum lists", i.e. whitespace-only values are rejected too.

One more link in the chain: the client's enum is under `allOf[0]`, not directly on `operationStatus`. `plainSchema` L978-1016 merges every `allOf` member's fields into the parent schema, which lifts it to `operationStatus.enum`; the `case "enum"` above then relays `""` unchanged. That is why Google's error path reads `...operationStatus.enum[3]` rather than `...operationStatus.allOf[0].enum[3]`. The fix can land either in the merge or in the enum case.

This is a gap in exactly the family of guards that already exists a few lines below: `items` is filled in for arrays (L1147-1170, #2826), type-specific keywords are stripped (L1172-1190, #3121/#3370/#3481), `const` is folded into a one-value enum (L1134-1138). Enum *values* are the only member-level check missing. `geminiToolTypes` in `internal/gateway/gemini_schema.go` only infers missing `type`s for Vertex and does not touch enum values either.

## Why this is magpie's to fix

- `enum: ["queued", "running", "done", ""]` is valid JSON Schema. Clients commonly use `""` as an "unset / no status" sentinel, and OpenAI- / Anthropic-compatible upstreams accept it — the same tool set works when routed to those providers. It is not a bad client config that the user should work around.
- magpie is the protocol-translation layer in front of a strict upstream. Every comparable strict-schema mismatch has been fixed in `plainSchema` (see related issues below); leaving one empty string in an enum is the same bug shape.
- The 400 is terminal and deterministic, but it is currently surfaced as a generic upstream failure, so routing/retry logic can burn attempts on an identical body.

## Suggested fix

In `plainSchema`'s `case "enum"`, drop members that are empty after trimming before writing `out[k]`, and keep the existing "all members are strings" guard (so numeric enums are still discarded rather than stringified). If the enum becomes empty, drop the `enum` keyword entirely instead of emitting `enum: []`. The same filter should apply wherever a schema is relayed as `parametersJsonSchema`, so the fix holds for the Gemini branch proposed in #3481 as well. Sketch:

```go
case "enum":
    if es, ok := v.([]any); ok {
        strs := make([]any, 0, len(es))
        for _, e := range es {
            s, ok := e.(string)
            if !ok { break }
            if strings.TrimSpace(s) == "" { continue } // Google: enum[i]: cannot be empty
            strs = append(strs, s)
        }
        if len(strs) > 0 { out[k] = strs }
    }
```

## Suggested minimal repro

A standalone minimal repro (the real occurrence is documented above; this one isolates the empty enum member from a 600-tool request), in the same shape as #3370. Point at a local gateway whose route resolves to an Antigravity account:

```bash
curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:3425/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Authorization: Bearer magpie' \
  -d '{
    "model": "antigravity/<gemini-model>",
    "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"reply OK"}],
    "tools": [{"type":"function","function":{
      "name":"probe","description":"probe",
      "parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{
        "StatusMessage":{"type":"object","properties":{
          "operationStatus":{"type":"string","enum":["queued","running","done",""]}
        }}
      }}
    }}]
  }'
```

Expected: HTTP 400 `...enum[3]: cannot be empty`. Removing the trailing `""` should return 200. That A/B pair isolates the empty enum member.

## Environment

- **magpie v0.1.2945** (published 2026-11-04, the latest release at filing time): https://github.com/yetone/magpie-releases/releases/tag/v0.1.2945
  - The main-repo tag `v0.1.2945` points at `43849d51c327` ("routing: By session heads a session by its title"), one commit after `0f1b40bbc413`. `internal/gateway/codeassist.go` is byte-identical between the two, so the `case "enum"` gap above is present unmodified in this build. The release notes list only that session-routing improvement — no schema fix.
- **Client / OS**: DeepSeek Harness (`dsh`) desktop on macOS.
- **Route / model**: a routing group → `antigravity/gemini-3.8-flash`, an Antigravity subscription. `effort` unset or `high` both fail; `effort=low` requests carry no tools and succeed.
- **MCP**: six instances of one Portainer MCP server, plus two small MCP servers. All six Portainer instances ship the same `StatusMessage` schema.
- Full untruncated error lines are in the Summary above. Account identifiers, host names and addresses, local paths, and the reporter's MCP-to-host mapping are deliberately omitted.

## Why one schema is reported six times (resolved)

This initially looked like magpie injecting one tool set four times (indices 24 / 129 / 234 / 339, stride 105). The routing log explains it: the reporter configured **six instances of the same Portainer MCP server**, each exposing ~105 tools including the same `StatusMessage` definition. Every server contributes one copy, so Google reports the same bad enum at ~105-tool intervals (24 / 129 / 234 / 339 / 474 / 607). **This is client configuration, not magpie duplicating tools** — `buildCodeAssistSent` appends each `r.Tools` entry once (L506-521). The unusually large tool set is a separate concern and is not the cause of this 400.

## Duplicate check

Searched the repo's issues before filing: `StatusMessage` → 0 hits, `operationStatus` → 0 hits, `"cannot be empty"` → 7 hits, none about tool schemas (they are about settings, deepseek passthrough, Codex compaction, etc.). `"function_declarations" "enum"` → #3121, #2826, #3173, all about union types / `items`, not empty enum values.

Related (same `plainSchema` hardening family, different trigger): #2179, #2826, #3121, #3173, #3370, #3481, #4242.
